mitting SNAPSHOT trees after the map was resolved against a now-superseded graph.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.2,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan step 3: 'After importing, reconcile retained SNAPSHOT payloads before finalizing the rewritten branch: use authorship_resolution.build_position_commit_map against the rewritten graph to replace every ledger position.commit_sha... Emit the updated tree through fast-import so C5 has no dangling preimage commit references.'","ADR 0106 C5 (docs/adr/0106-...md:151-155): 'Where an above-horizon SNAPSHOT embeds a below-horizon commit_sha... that reference is rewritten during collapse — this is the interleaved reconciliation core (story S1), not a sequential post-pass.'","build_position_commit_map (authorship_resolution.py:292) maps position->OLDEST commit that added the .json; re-emitting a SNAPSHOT tree changes downstream commit SHAs, so a map built before the re-emit can be stale for positions at/after the mutated commit."],"finding":"Step 3 sequences the SNAPSHOT ledger reconciliation as a post-import pass ('After importing... use build_position_commit_map against the rewritten graph... Emit the updated tree through fast-import'), but rewriting a SNAPSHOT tree's commit_sha annotations changes that commit's tree and hence its SHA, invalidating the position->commit_sha map that was resolved against the pre-reconciliation graph. ADR 0106 C5 explicitly requires this to be interleaved into the collapse, 'NOT a sequential post-pass.'","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc70eada2012dc6fb","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Implementation plan, step 3 (and step 2 stream construction)","norm_id":"n0bdccb3e0eb4ca64","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A retained above-horizon SNAPSHOT embeds a below-horizon position.commit_sha; the collapse rewrites the graph, the map is built, the SNAPSHOT tree is re-emitted with new 